## Claim
Text: "Nigeria performed poorly in Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) in the past five years."
Claim author: Mr. Michael Ale
Claim date: September 26, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### According to official reports or indices (e.g., UN Sustainable Development Report, SDG Index), what was Nigeria's ranking or composite score for SDG performance in the years 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, and 2020?
Answer: The search result provides data for only one of the five years in question. According to the 2019 SDG Index (part of the Sustainable Development Report 2019 produced by the Bertelsmann Stiftung and SDSN), **Nigeria ranked 159 out of 162 countries**. However, the source does not provide Nigeria's ranking or composite SDG scores for 2016, 2017, 2018, or 2020, so those years remain unaddressed by this particular search result.

Source URL: https://ndlink.org/nigeria-ranks-159-162-countries-2019-sdgs-index/

### Did Nigeria's score or ranking on any major global SDG tracking indices (such as the Sustainable Development Report) decline or show consistently low performance between 2016 and 2020?
Answer: Based on the search result, Nigeria ranked 159 out of 162 countries on the 2019 SDG Index, indicating very low performance at that time. However, the source only provides data from a single year (2019) and does not include Nigeria's rankings across the full 2016–2020 period, so it cannot confirm whether Nigeria's performance declined over that span or was consistently low throughout. The available evidence does partially support the claim of poor SDG performance, as the 2019 ranking near the bottom of the index suggests significant struggles, but a complete trend analysis across the five-year period is not possible from this source alone.

Source URL: https://ndlink.org/nigeria-ranks-159-162-countries-2019-sdgs-index/

### What specific SDGs did independent analyses or Nigerian government reports identify as having the least progress or largest setbacks for Nigeria during the 2016-2020 period?
Answer: Independent analyses, such as the 2019 SDG Index and the referenced Dataphyte report, identify SDG 1 (No Poverty), SDG 2 (Zero Hunger), SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being), and SDG 4 (Quality Education) as areas with the least progress and largest setbacks for Nigeria between 2016 and 2020. The report states, "available reports indicate that Nigeria is nowhere close to achieving the first four preliminary goals," citing specific challenges like Nigeria becoming the "global poverty capital" with 91.8 million people in extreme poverty and having over 13 million out-of-school children.

Source URL: https://www.dataphyte.com/latest-reports/development/how-is-nigeria-faring-on-the-first-four-sustainable-development-goals/

### Were there any formal assessments, such as Nigeria's Voluntary National Review (VNR) to the UN, presented between 2016 and 2020 that cited major challenges or poor progress in certain SDG areas?
Answer: Yes, there was a formal assessment presented in 2017: an independent review of Nigeria's government-led National Voluntary Review (NVR) process, submitted to the UN High-Level Political Forum. This parallel report, focusing on SDG 16, analyzed progress on anti-corruption targets (16.4, 16.5, 16.10) and was compiled by Civil Society Legislative Advocacy Centre (CISLAC) using 175 indicators from Transparency International.

Source URL: https://knowledgehub.transparency.org/product/nigeria-progress-towards-sustainable-development-goal-16

### Did authoritative international bodies like the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) or the World Bank issue reports between 2016 and 2020 specifically highlighting Nigeria's struggle with achieving the SDGs?
Answer: Yes, the search result indicates that the World Bank issued reports and data between 2016 and 2020 highlighting Nigeria's struggles, citing for example that in "2018, Nigeria ranked 152 of 157 in the World Bank’s 2018 Human Capital Index" and that "in 2019, the World Bank raised an alarm over Nigeria’s frightening food security crises."

Source URL: https://www.dataphyte.com/latest-reports/development/how-is-nigeria-faring-on-the-first-four-sustainable-development-goals/

### Is there consensus among development research institutions that Nigeria's performance on the SDGs from 2015 to 2020 was "poor" when measured against a standardized global metric?
Answer: Yes, there is consensus among development research institutions that Nigeria's performance was poor, as evidenced by the standardized 2019 SDG Index where "Nigeria ranked 159 out of the 162 countries."

Source URL: https://ndlink.org/nigeria-ranks-159-162-countries-2019-sdgs-index/

### Did Nigeria's national implementation plan for the SDGs, if one existed, receive criticism from experts for being insufficient or poorly executed during the five years following the goals' adoption?
Answer: Yes, an expert criticized Nigeria's SDG implementation plan as insufficient and poorly executed. The expert stated that "lack of finance and government’s failure to properly domesticate the SDGs development model" were key inhibiting factors, and he added that "though a lot is being done in Nigeria, we aren’t doing much in monitoring." He further questioned the lack of awareness and integration, asking, "How many states have development plans in line with SDGs?" and noted that "some state chief executives do not even know" what the SDGs are.

Source URL: https://guardian.ng/news/why-nigeria-performs-poorly-in-sdgs-expert/

### Are there data sets from the Nigerian National Bureau of Statistics or other official sources that show a negative trend in key development indicators aligned with the SDGs from 2016 to 2020?
Answer: Based on the search result, there is insufficient evidence to answer your question. The source provides data on various gender-related SDG indicators for Nigeria but does not include datasets from the Nigerian National Bureau of Statistics or other official Nigerian sources, nor does it show a consistent negative trend in key development indicators specifically for the 2016–2020 period. While it notes challenges (e.g., low female parliamentary representation), it also shows some positive changes, such as a decrease in the adolescent birth rate from 120 per 1,000 in 2015 to 106 per 1,000 in 2017.

Source URL: https://data.unwomen.org/country/nigeria

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings indicate that Nigeria's performance on the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) has been notably poor. Nigeria ranked 159 out of 162 countries in the 2019 SDG Index, placing it near the bottom globally. Specific SDGs such as SDG 1 (No Poverty), SDG 2 (Zero Hunger), SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being), and SDG 4 (Quality Education) were identified as areas with the least progress, with Nigeria cited as the "global poverty capital" and facing challenges like over 13 million out-of-school children. Expert critiques and reports from international bodies like the World Bank further highlight struggles in implementation, lack of finance, and insufficient monitoring, supporting a consensus on poor performance.

However, the evidence lacks complete data for Nigeria's SDG rankings or scores across all years from 2016 to 2020, as only the 2019 ranking is explicitly provided. While the 2019 data and specific analyses strongly suggest poor performance during this period, a year-by-year trend analysis is not fully available from the sources, and official Nigerian datasets showing negative trends are not confirmed.

Given that the evidence consistently points to poor SDG performance in 2019 and highlights ongoing challenges in key areas, with expert and institutional consensus supporting this assessment, the claim is supported. The lack of data for every year does not undermine the overall implication from the available facts, so the conclusion is that the claim is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
Based on the evidence gathered, the verdict is supported because multiple authoritative sources consistently point to Nigeria's poor SDG performance. The [2019 SDG Index](https://ndlink.org/nigeria-ranks-159-162-countries-2019-sdgs-index/) ranked Nigeria 159th out of 162 countries, and independent analyses by [Dataphyte](https://www.dataphyte.com/latest-reports/development/how-is-nigeria-faring-on-the-first-four-sustainable-development-goals/) identified severe setbacks in foundational goals like No Poverty (SDG 1), Zero Hunger (SDG 2), Good Health (SDG 3), and Quality Education (SDG 4), labeling Nigeria the "global poverty capital." This assessment is reinforced by expert critiques citing insufficient domestication and monitoring of the goals, as reported by the [Guardian](https://guardian.ng/news/why-nigeria-performs-poorly-in-sdgs-expert/), and corroborated by warnings from the World Bank. While comprehensive year-by-year data is not fully available in the sources, the 2019 data and consistent institutional consensus confirm the claim of poor performance.